Subject: pre-size ESTree JSON maps

Issue: ubugeeei-prod/uf#668
Upstream: not filed with facebook/flow yet
Submodule: 81b0c2a3dd591c66c51167aac341d851932bd9c5

The remaining allocation-heavy part of the #668 lint path is the Flow parser's
ESTree renderer: it walks a typed AST and builds a `serde_json::Value` tree for
the React Compiler. uf can avoid that renderer when a lint rule does not need
the compiler's Babel-shaped tree, but real compiler work still has to render it.

Every ESTree node is a JSON object whose field count is known before insertion:
`type`, optional comment fields, optional `range`/`loc`, and the caller's own
properties. The translator was creating those objects with `Map::new()`, then
letting the backing index map grow while the known fields were inserted. The
small generic object helper and the nested `loc` position objects did the same.
The hottest node builders also assembled those known properties in a temporary
`Vec`, one heap allocation before the map was even built.

This gives each map the capacity the caller already knows, and lets fixed
property lists use arrays instead of a temporary `Vec`. It preserves key order
and values exactly; measured with:

    cargo run --release --example alloc_report -p uf_transform -- \
      npm/router/internal/runtime.js
    cargo run --release --example alloc_report -p uf_transform -- \
      npm/router/internal/runtime.js --dump

on the #668 router runtime fixture:

    babel_ast allocations/run    324,115 -> 305,766  (-5.7%)
    bytes allocated/run        34.89 MiB -> 29.38 MiB (-15.8%)

The dumped Babel tree stayed byte-identical:

    74fccbca85fadb849a21be76f31559b5a07fedf010c86a5c53384e0e0304bd8d

